WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.719 --> 00:00:06.620
of the things that Donald Trump has said a
number of times now to try to convince people

00:00:06.620 --> 00:00:11.480
that impeaching him is a bad idea is he's
been saying the stock market would crash if

00:00:11.480 --> 00:00:12.800
I got impeached.

00:00:12.800 --> 00:00:15.830
Now, it's not a crazy thing to suggest.

00:00:15.830 --> 00:00:18.009
Markets in general don't like instability.

00:00:18.009 --> 00:00:24.239
The removal of a president or even the attempted
removal of a president is a form of instability,

00:00:24.239 --> 00:00:29.710
so it is reasonable to think, wow, if Trump
were impeached, if Trump were impeached and

00:00:29.710 --> 00:00:35.770
convicted for removal, the markets would absolutely
crash and Donald Trump has kept pushing this

00:00:35.770 --> 00:00:36.770
lately.

00:00:36.770 --> 00:00:41.610
He tweeted late last week, quote, if they
actually did this, the markets would crash.

00:00:41.610 --> 00:00:46.770
Do you think it was luck that got us to the
best stock market and economy in our history?

00:00:46.770 --> 00:00:52.390
It wasn't so put aside for a second that most
stocks are held by relatively few people,

00:00:52.390 --> 00:00:58.770
Richard people, and so the level of the stock
market is not the best indicator of the health

00:00:58.770 --> 00:00:59.770
of the economy.

00:00:59.770 --> 00:01:01.290
Put that aside just for a second.

00:01:01.290 --> 00:01:02.290
Okay.

00:01:02.290 --> 00:01:06.170
There's a good case to be made that markets
would actually do really well and might even

00:01:06.170 --> 00:01:09.480
sore rather than crash if Donald Trump were
impeached.

00:01:09.480 --> 00:01:13.310
Rick Newman from Yahoo finance makes the case
for this, and Rick points out that number

00:01:13.310 --> 00:01:17.300
one, markets have been relatively stable since
the impeachment inquiry was announced.

00:01:17.300 --> 00:01:22.140
You've actually seen very few big swing days
one way or the other since the impeachment

00:01:22.140 --> 00:01:23.650
inquiry was announced.

00:01:23.650 --> 00:01:28.909
That's the first sign that we should question
this so-called conventional wisdom of impeachment

00:01:28.909 --> 00:01:34.250
is terrible for the markets, but Rick Newman
actually says markets would soar if Trump

00:01:34.250 --> 00:01:40.270
was impeached because the things that Donald
Trump has done, which tend to boost stocks.

00:01:40.270 --> 00:01:41.350
Now this is for better or worse.

00:01:41.350 --> 00:01:46.420
This is not me saying I agree with these policies,
but Trump has done tax cuts and deregulation

00:01:46.420 --> 00:01:47.430
very often.

00:01:47.430 --> 00:01:48.580
That boosts stocks.

00:01:48.580 --> 00:01:50.290
Of course, there's [inaudible].

00:01:50.290 --> 00:01:53.350
There's an expense to to that as well, which
we can talk about.

00:01:53.350 --> 00:01:57.820
Those would in principle stay if Donald Trump
were removed, but a lot of the bad things

00:01:57.820 --> 00:02:04.420
Trump has done would likely end the tariffs,
the xenophobia interfering with free markets.

00:02:04.420 --> 00:02:05.979
Even on things like immigration.

00:02:05.979 --> 00:02:07.920
Immigration is great for the economy.

00:02:07.920 --> 00:02:13.590
The gains from immigration, even including
undocumented immigration, are far greater

00:02:13.590 --> 00:02:15.280
than the costs to the economy.

00:02:15.280 --> 00:02:18.320
Now, this all has a cost to it, right?

00:02:18.320 --> 00:02:22.360
Low wages, poor conditions for many legal
and illegal immigrants.

00:02:22.360 --> 00:02:24.660
I'm not making a moral analysis here.

00:02:24.660 --> 00:02:29.380
I'm merely making an economic one for the
sake of discussion and there is a strong case

00:02:29.380 --> 00:02:34.150
to be made that if Donald Trump were impeached
and removed, it would not crash the stock

00:02:34.150 --> 00:02:36.410
market the way Donald Trump is pointing out.

00:02:36.410 --> 00:02:42.380
Now there is a bigger argument which is if
the right thing to do for legal reasons, on

00:02:42.380 --> 00:02:47.340
the basis of the evidence for moral reasons
is to impeach a president.

00:02:47.340 --> 00:02:48.340
You do it.

00:02:48.340 --> 00:02:52.030
You don't make the calculation that in the
short term the market won't like it, so we

00:02:52.030 --> 00:02:54.930
let a president remain president who shouldn't
be president.

00:02:54.930 --> 00:02:56.660
That's a separate argument.

00:02:56.660 --> 00:03:00.970
What we're talking about merely is the practicality
and the logistics of the market will crash

00:03:00.970 --> 00:03:03.450
versus the market will soar if Trump is impeached.

00:03:03.450 --> 00:03:05.260
Let me give you another example.

00:03:05.260 --> 00:03:07.080
The craziness with Iran.

00:03:07.080 --> 00:03:13.349
What's bad for markets is the instability
with Iran, which would probably calm down

00:03:13.349 --> 00:03:15.840
if Donald Trump was removed from office.

00:03:15.840 --> 00:03:21.310
Now Rick Newman is also saying that if Trump
were replaced with Pence, the economy would

00:03:21.310 --> 00:03:22.739
be much better off.

00:03:22.739 --> 00:03:27.830
I find Pence disgusting and it would be terrible,
but the main point here that Rick Newman is

00:03:27.830 --> 00:03:34.100
making and which I think is is a valid point
to make or at least to propose, is that Donald

00:03:34.100 --> 00:03:39.400
Trump's removal would not be terrible for
the economy as a foregone conclusion and we

00:03:39.400 --> 00:03:44.150
need to challenge all of those ideas merely
because they might've been accurate 20 years

00:03:44.150 --> 00:03:45.150
ago.

00:03:45.150 --> 00:03:49.510
Merely because it seems to make some intuitive
sense does not mean that we should fall forward,

00:03:49.510 --> 00:03:52.760
hook, line and sinker and more importantly
one way or the other.

00:03:52.760 --> 00:03:54.359
This is not the basis on which impeachment
should be decided upon.

